## Key Findings (what the research changes about the plan)

1. **The repo is public. That helps and hurts.** HUNT-001/DriftSense is public.\[1\] The GitHub docs say rulesets "are available in public repositories with GitHub Free", so you can enforce protection without paying.\[2\] The `ubuntu-24.04-arm` standard runner is free on public repos with 4 vCPUs.\[3\] GitHub's Jan 29, 2026 changelog ("arm64 standard runners are now available in private repositories") says these runners also work in private repos: "In private repositories, these runners have two vCPUs; in public repositories, they have four vCPUs", and private-repo use counts toward your plan's free minutes. **The downside: SEALED test data must never be committed.** Keep it in a private S3 bucket that only Person A's SSO role can read. Commit only the protocol and its hash.
2. **Do NOT turn on "Require review from Code Owners".** GitHub won't let a PR author approve their own PR, and in this repo each directory has a single owner who is also the usual author. So most PRs would have no eligible code-owner reviewer and could never merge. (This is my inference from how GitHub reviews work, so check it in the ruleset UI.) Keep CODEOWNERS for automatic review requests and as ownership documentation, and require 1 approval of any kind.
3. **"Require approval of the most recent reviewable push" will slow you down.** Strict up-to-date checks force a rebase each time the other person merges, and this setting then demands a fresh approval after every rebase. Leave it off. Turn on "dismiss stale approvals" instead, which GitHub applies only "when commits are pushed that affect the diff". A clean rebase therefore keeps the approval.\[4\]
4. **Claude Code's memory is per machine, so anything the other machine needs has to be in git.** Claude Code loads `CLAUDE.md` and `CLAUDE.local.md` from the working directory and every directory above it. The docs say to add `CLAUDE.local.md` to `.gitignore`.\[5\] Auto memory lives in `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/` and is never shared.\[6\] Handoffs must therefore go through PR bodies, GitHub issues or committed docs, never through "Claude remembers".
5. **Hooks enforce the rules; CLAUDE.md only states them.** The hooks guide says hooks give "deterministic control: certain actions always happen rather than relying on the LLM to choose to run them".\[7\] A `PreToolUse` hook that exits 2 blocks the tool call, and its stderr goes back to Claude as feedback.\[7\] The permissions docs add that a blocking hook "takes precedence over allow rules".\[8\] Ownership and the ban on pushing to `main` therefore go in hooks, not just in prose.
6. **Settings layering lets you share policy and keep personal tweaks.** `.claude/settings.json` is committed. `.claude/settings.local.json` is personal and wins on conflicts.\[9\] Per the settings docs, permission arrays are merged across scopes.\[10\] Custom commands in `.claude/commands/` still work. The docs call them legacy in favour of `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, but both give you `/name`.\[11\]\[12\]
7. **Deadline arithmetic.** Devpost says final projects are due "October 26, 2026, at 11:59 p.m." Pacific.\[13\]\[14\]\[15\] US daylight saving ends Nov 1, 2026, so that is PDT (UTC−7) = **Oct 27 12:29 IST**. Your 12:15 IST hard stop is a sensible 14-minute buffer. Aim to submit at 21:00 IST on Oct 26.
8. **Using Claude Code does not count toward the Agentic Vision award.** Devpost states: "Using an AI coding assistant to write the entry does not qualify as an agentic workflow". The award also requires "a trace or demonstration showing that OpenCV 5 output changes a later decision, tool call, or action".\[14\]\[16\] The trace schema below is built to show exactly that: margin vs. tau → commit or escalate.

