## Details

### 1. Git workflow rules (put these in CLAUDE.md §Git)

**Model: trunk-based development with short-lived branches.** The AWS Well-Architected DevOps Guidance ([DL.SCM.2] "Keep feature branches short-lived", a foundational best practice) says: "We recommend trunk-based development paired with a pull request workflow utilizing short-lived feature branches as the most effective branching strategy when practicing DevOps." On top of that, this plan asks each person to merge to `main` at least once a day. Long-lived per-person branches (`personA`, `personB`) would put all the integration risk into Oct 16–20, when you have the least slack. Reject them.

| Rule | Setting |
|---|---|
| Branch names | `a/p<phase>-<topic>`, `b/p<phase>-<topic>`, `shared/<topic>` (shared files only), `int/g<gate>-<topic>` (integration), `fix/<topic>` (after freeze), `docs/<topic>` |
| Branch lifetime | ≤ 2 days; open a draft PR on day 1 |
| Base | always `origin/main`; never branch from another person's branch (no stacked PRs across people) |
| Update policy | `git fetch && git rebase origin/main` on your **own** branch only; push with `--force-with-lease`; never rebase someone else's branch |
| Merge method | squash only (ruleset `allowed_merge_methods: ["squash"]` + linear history) |
| Reviews | 1 approval from the other person; Claude drafts the review and the human submits it under their own `gh` account |
| Commit messages | Conventional style: `feat(v2/lattice): …`, `fix(service): …`, `chore(shared): …`; the squash title is the PR title |
| Unfinished work | merge behind the engine registry/env flag (`DRIFTSENSE_ENGINE`), not on long branches |
| Shared files | edited only on `shared/*` branches, announced at sync, merged **before** that day's feature PRs |
| Tags | annotated, created only by humans via `/tag-gate` (the hook blocks agents otherwise) |
| Stacked PRs | avoid them. If you do stack, don't use `--delete-branch` on the base PR. GitHub issue #1781 in Claudfather/Claudlobby shows why: PR #1776 was merged with `gh pr merge <n> --squash --admin --delete-branch` at 11:53:01Z, and the PR stacked on it (#1779) "went CLOSED at 11:53:03Z, two seconds later". Retarget dependent PRs first with `gh pr edit <m> --base main` |

**Ownership map** (this is what CODEOWNERS and the edit hook both use):

| Area | Owner | Steward for conflicts |
|---|---|---|
| `driftsense/v1/` (frozen), `driftsense/v2/`, `driftsense/sim/`, `driftsense/agent/`, `driftsense/engines/`, `eval/`, `tests/engine/`, `tests/agent/`, `docs/REPORT.md`, `docs/virtual_sem.md`, `docs/PRIOR_WORK.md`, `results/accuracy/`, `results/sealed/`, `results/agent/` | A | A |
| `service/`, `infra/`, `bench/`, `ui/`, `scripts/`, `tests/service/`, `tests/infra/`, other `docs/*`, `results/bench/`, `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` | B | B |
| `driftsense/contracts.py`, `driftsense/api.py`, `contracts/`, `tests/contract/` | shared | **A** |
| `requirements.in`, `requirements*.lock`, `Makefile`, `.github/` (CI, rulesets, CODEOWNERS, template) | shared | **B** |
| `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/`, `pyproject.toml`, `.gitignore` | shared | **A** |

**Conflict ownership.** Whoever merges second rebases and resolves. In files you own, you resolve. In shared files, the steward resolves, or at least approves the resolution. Lockfiles are never merged by hand: take main's version and regenerate (§6).

